<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Pilot light diagnostic</p>
    <h2>Thermocouple weakness is the #1 cause across all commercial gas equipment. Year 4 to 7 typical.</h2>

    <p>A commercial range pilot light that won't stay lit, blows out during service, or shows weak pilot flame in LA commercial kitchens usually traces to one of three root causes our techs at Same Day Appliance Repair diagnose first across LA steakhouse corridor (Mastro's, STK, Ruth's Chris), DTLA hotel banquet kitchens (JW Marriott, Ritz-Carlton), and San Gabriel Valley ethnic restaurant cook lines running Vulcan VR, Garland Master Series, Imperial IR/IHR, Wolf Commercial Challenger XL, and Montague Legend standing-pilot gas ranges. (1) Thermocouple weakness year 4-7 — single most common pilot failure across all commercial gas equipment. Millivolt output drops below 14mV threshold under full pilot flame, can't hold the gas safety valve open. $220 to $340 replacement, 20-40 minutes. (2) Gas valve failure year 8-12 — primary gas control sticks closed or fails to modulate, no gas flow to pilot regardless of thermocouple signal. Requires BHGS-licensed plus CSLB C-20 HVAC work plus CARB + LADBS commercial gas equipment compliance documentation. $260 to $440 replacement. (3) Pilot orifice carbon clog from LA dust + grease accumulation — restricts gas flow to pilot, weak or unstable flame. $120 to $200 cleaning service, often resolves at $120 commercial diagnostic without parts. Plus drafty kitchen exhaust hood pulling pilot off thermocouple ($60-$120 pilot-shield kit) and gas pressure fluctuation across manifold during peak demand ($200-$600 regulator-side fix). 5-step cheap-first diagnostic at the $120 commercial diagnostic identifies which mode applies.</p>

    <p>Pilot light failures on commercial ranges (and across all standing-pilot commercial gas equipment) cluster around five mechanisms. (1) Thermocouple weak or failing, year 4 to 7 typical, $220 to $340 replacement. Single most common cause. (2) Thermopile failure (millivolt-system equipment), year 5 to 8, $260 to $440. (3) Drafty kitchen exhaust hood pulling pilot off thermocouple, $60 to $120 pilot-shield kit as quick fix plus mechanical-contractor referral for makeup-air balancing. (4) Gas pressure fluctuation across manifold during peak demand, $200 to $600 regulator-side fix. (5) Pilot orifice carbon clog, $120 to $200 cleaning service.</p>

    <p>The pro-tip framing: annual thermocouple replacement before peak season is the highest-leverage PM action on commercial gas equipment. For LA restaurants with 4+ pieces of standing-pilot equipment, the math strongly favors annual PM ($1,400 to $4,000 across the kitchen) over reactive emergency dispatch when a thermocouple fails mid-service ($1,200 to $4,000 lost revenue per Friday or Saturday plus dispatch fee).</p>

    <p>$120 commercial diagnostic, waived with repair. <strong>BHGS #A49573</strong>. <strong>EPA 608 Universal certified #1346255700410</strong>. BBB Accredited. Phones answered 24/7. Parent: <a href="/commercial/range-repair/">commercial range repair</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Field observations</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Top causes by frequency.</h2>
    <p>Across LA County commercial range pilot light service calls our techs at Same Day Appliance Repair dispatch — LA steakhouse corridor running Vulcan VR + Garland Master Series + Wolf Commercial Challenger XL, DTLA hotel banquet kitchens with standing-pilot legacy installations, San Gabriel Valley ethnic restaurant cook lines with continuous-operation Vulcan VR-48/60/72, LAUSD school nutrition + healthcare cafeteria institutional, plus mid-tier restaurant operations with legacy standing-pilot gas equipment — the failure distribution clusters predictably. About 50 percent thermocouple weakness (year 4-7, $220-$340 replacement, single most common pilot failure across all commercial gas equipment). 20 percent gas valve failure (year 8-12, $260-$440 BHGS+CSLB C-20 plus CARB + LADBS documentation). 15 percent pilot orifice carbon clog from LA dust + grease ($120-$200 cleaning, often resolves at $120 commercial diagnostic without parts). 10 percent drafty exhaust hood pulling pilot off thermocouple ($60-$120 pilot-shield kit + makeup-air mechanical contractor referral). 5 percent gas pressure fluctuation across manifold during peak demand ($200-$600 regulator-side fix).</p>

    <ol class="num-list">
      <li><strong>Thermocouple weak / failing (year 4-7).</strong> Single most common pilot failure. Output drops below threshold to hold gas valve open. $220-340 replacement.</li>
      <li><strong>Pilot orifice carbon clog (year 3-5).</strong> Pilot lights weak or won't light. $120-200 cleaning.</li>
      <li><strong>Thermopile failure (millivolt-system equipment, year 5-8).</strong> $260-440 on premium models.</li>
      <li><strong>Drafty kitchen exhaust pulling pilot off thermocouple.</strong> Hood-makeup-air engineering issue. $60-120 pilot-shield kit + mechanical-contractor referral.</li>
      <li><strong>Gas pressure fluctuation across manifold.</strong> $200-600 regulator-side fix.</li>
      <li><strong>Pilot tube physical damage (bent, kinked).</strong> $180-280 repair.</li>
      <li><strong>Gas valve magnetic latch wear (year 8+).</strong> Valve closes intermittently even with adequate thermocouple signal. $480-680 replacement.</li>
      <li><strong>Pilot light extinguishing during cleaning protocols.</strong> Operator education / pilot relight protocols.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Diagnostic walkthrough</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Practitioner sequence.</h2>
    <ol class="num-list">
      <li><strong>Identify ignition architecture.</strong> Standing pilot (continuous flame) vs IPI (intermittent pilot ignition, lights only when called).</li>
      <li><strong>Visual pilot flame strength and steadiness.</strong> Strong steady blue flame indicates pilot is fine; weak yellow flame indicates orifice issue or pressure issue.</li>
      <li><strong>Multimeter thermocouple under flame.</strong> Voltage output during pilot, target 18-30 mV holding, below 14 mV indicates failure.</li>
      <li><strong>Visual draft test (suspected exhaust issue).</strong> Pilot flicker pattern correlated with hood operation, makeup-air balance.</li>
      <li><strong>Manometer pressure during peak demand.</strong> Manifold pressure with all kitchen equipment running.</li>
      <li><strong>Pilot orifice inspection.</strong> Visual carbon buildup, compressed-air cleaning if visible deposits.</li>
      <li><strong>Pilot tube physical inspection.</strong> Bent, kinked, or partially closed tube.</li>
      <li><strong>Gas valve magnetic latch test.</strong> Mechanical actuation under thermocouple voltage simulation.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Honest opinion</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Annual thermocouple PM is the smartest restaurant maintenance investment.</h2>
    <p>Most LA restaurants we serve adopt annual thermocouple PM only after one expensive reactive failure cost them $2,000+ in lost Friday or Saturday revenue. The math is obvious in retrospect: a $300 thermocouple replacement scheduled in advance during low-volume hours is cheaper than the same replacement during dinner rush plus all the lost revenue, customer experience damage, and labor of operating around a partial-out range. Some shops won't recommend this PM because reactive emergency calls are higher margin. We recommend it because it's what we'd do for our own restaurant. The cost-benefit is clear after the first incident.</p>
  </div>
</section>

